#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# position_largest

#% text
# `position_largest(xs)` gives the positions of the largest elements of `xs` in numerical order.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Every position with the largest value is given. `position_largest(xs, n)` gives the positions of the `n` largest distinct values, one list per value, largest first. An association gives the positions of its values.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
position_largest([1, 3, 2])
#% output
#= [2]

#% cell
position_largest([1, 3, 3])
#% output
#= [2, 3]

#% cell
position_largest([pi, e, 3])
#% output
#= [1]

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# The three largest values:

#% cell
position_largest([100, 200, 100, 200, 50], 3)
#% output
#= [[4, 2], [3, 1], [5]]

#% cell
position_largest([-1.5, -0.5, -2])
#% output
#= [2]

#% cell
position_largest([])
#% output
#= []

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `position_smallest`, `max`, `ordering`, `take_largest`
